{
  "schemaVersion": 2,
  "id": "market-regulations-cybersecurity",
  "title": "Cybersecurity Rules",
  "turns": [
    {
      "jobId": "dda6fa32-d728-4698-902a-f7cc715eb8ba",
      "question": "What does Title 17 of the eCFR require public companies to disclose about material cybersecurity incidents and cybersecurity risk management, strategy, and governance? Cite the relevant sections.",
      "submittedAt": "2026-09-29T20:30:33.478994Z",
      "completedAt": "2026-09-29T20:32:04.260671Z",
      "status": "success",
      "report": {
        "markdown": "**Title 17 CFR (eCFR) – Regulation S‑K, Item 106 (17 CFR § 229.106) – Cybersecurity**\n\nThe rule requires registrants (public companies) to disclose three linked topics:\n\n| Topic | What must be disclosed | Regulatory text |\n|-------|------------------------|-----------------|\n| **Definitions** | “Cybersecurity incident” = an unauthorized occurrence (or series) on or through the registrant’s information systems that jeopardizes confidentiality, integrity, or availability of those systems or the information they hold. “Cybersecurity threat” = any potential unauthorized occurrence that may cause adverse effects. “Information systems” = electronic resources owned or used by the registrant. | § 229.106(a) |\n| **Risk‑management & strategy** | 1. Describe the processes for **assessing, identifying, and managing material risks from cybersecurity threats** in enough detail for a reasonable investor to understand them.  <br>  • Whether those processes are integrated into the overall risk‑management system. <br>  • Whether the registrant uses assessors, consultants, auditors, or other third parties. <br>  • Whether the registrant has processes to oversee and identify risks from third‑party service providers. <br>2. Disclose **whether any cybersecurity‑threat risks (including from prior incidents) have materially affected—or are reasonably likely to materially affect—the registrant’s business strategy, results of operations, or financial condition**, and explain how. | § 229.106(b)(1)–(2) |\n| **Governance** | 1. **Board oversight** – describe the board’s oversight of cybersecurity‑threat risks; identify any board committee/sub‑committee responsible and the processes by which the board or committee is informed. <br>2. **Management’s role** – describe which management positions or committees are responsible for assessing and managing material cybersecurity risks, their relevant expertise, the processes by which they are informed about and monitor prevention, detection, mitigation, and remediation of incidents, and whether they report such information to the board or a board committee. | § 229.106(c)(1)–(2) |\n| **Structured‑data filing** | All Item 106 information must be provided in an Interactive Data File (Inline XBRL) per Rule 405 of Regulation S‑T and the EDGAR Filer Manual. | § 229.106(d) |\n\n**In practice** this means a public company must:\n\n* Define what it considers a “material cybersecurity incident” and “threat.”\n* Explain its cybersecurity risk‑management program, integration with enterprise risk management, use of third‑party experts, and oversight of vendor‑related cyber risk.\n* State whether any cyber risk (including past incidents) has had, or is likely to have, a material impact on its business, operations, or finances.\n* Detail board‑level oversight (committees, reporting lines) and management’s responsibility, expertise, and reporting processes for preventing, detecting, mitigating, and remediating incidents.\n* Submit the disclosures in structured (Inline XBRL) format.\n\nThese requirements are codified in **17 CFR § 229.106 (Item 106) – Cybersecurity** (see §§ 229.106(a)–(d))[1].\n\n## Sources\n\n- [1] Unstructured Retrieval evidence — 8 documents — evidence `hermes-receipt:a3c32b8e6a05dee461bb9d5e7df60052bcb2521de6e83dfa6494e8bba48e7153`",
